.TH PINBOARD 1
.SH NAME
pinboard \- desktop surface for the window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/pinboard
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pinboard
is a frameless, full-screen, always-bottom window that owns the
.IR wm (1)
desktop. Icons are pinned to arbitrary positions on it; the pinboard
can carry a backdrop image; and it is scripted both interactively
(by dragging icons from
.IR wm-filer (1)
or by clicks on its own canvas) and by writing a small line-oriented
text protocol to
.BR /chan/pinboard .
.PP
The design is loosely modelled on the RISC OS 3.7 pinboard. Each
pinned icon is just a link: a double-click on one runs the
associated application directly (for
.B !App
folders, by sourcing
.B !Boot
and executing
.B !Run
under a fresh namespace) or hands the path to
.IR plumber (1)
otherwise.
.PP
.I Pinboard
is normally started from
.B /lib/wmsetup
by the line
.PP
.RS
.B wmrun wm/pinboard
.RE
.PP
right after
.IR plumber (1)
is brought up, so that toolbar's
.B "#s"
mount on
.B /chan
is the parent of pinboard's namespace and the
.B /chan/pinboard
entry it creates is visible to every other wm-launched application.
.PP
.I Pinboard
also relies on the
.B Sticky
flag supported by the modified
.IR wm (1)
in this tree: the flag tells the wm not to auto-raise the pinboard
when the pointer lands on it, so user clicks on bare desktop don't
bring the pinboard above the toolbar or any other window.
.SH "TEXT PROTOCOL"
Pinboard serves a single
.IR file2chan (2)
file at
.BR /chan/pinboard .
Each write may carry one or more newline-terminated commands; writes
that don't end on a newline are buffered until the next write. Lines
that don't begin with one of the verbs below are silently ignored.
.TP
.BI pin " path " "[ x y]"
Place an icon for
.I path
on the pinboard. If
.I x
and
.I y
are present they are screen-relative pixel coordinates of the
icon's centre; otherwise the icon is auto-placed in the next free
slot of a row-major grid. The icon image is chosen by extension/type
the same way
.IR wm-filer (1)
does:
.B !App
folders use their own
.BI icons/ basename .bit
icon, plain directories use
.BR dir.bit ,
.B .dis
files use
.BR dis.bit ,
the image formats
.RB ( .bit ,
.BR .mask ,
.BR .gif ,
.BR .jpg ,
.BR .jpeg ,
.BR .png )
use
.BR pic.bit ,
and everything else uses
.BR file.bit .
.TP
.BI unpin " path"
Remove the pinned icon whose path is
.IR path .
Silently ignored if no such pin exists.
.TP
.BI backdrop " imgpath"
Replace the desktop backdrop with the image at
.IR imgpath .
Only Inferno
.B .bit
files are supported in this release; other formats need conversion
first (see
.IR image (6)).
.TP
.B clear
Remove every pinned icon. The backdrop is preserved.
.PP
A read from
.B /chan/pinboard
returns the current state in the same protocol, suitable for
saving to a file and writing back later:
.PP
.RS
.EX
% cp /chan/pinboard $home/lib/pinboard
.\" later, after a fresh wm or `clear`:
% cp $home/lib/pinboard /chan/pinboard
.EE
.RE
.PP
The first line of the dump is a
.B backdrop
line if one is in effect, followed by one
.B pin
line per icon (in insertion order).
.PP
Example: place a Calc icon at (200, 200), then a file icon, then
remove the first:
.PP
.RS
.EX
% echo 'pin /usr/inferno/!Calc 200 200' >/chan/pinboard
% echo 'pin /NOTICE'                   >/chan/pinboard
% echo 'unpin /usr/inferno/!Calc'      >/chan/pinboard
.EE
.RE
.SH MOUSE
Within the pinboard, button bindings are:
.TP 8
.B B1
on an icon: select it (replacing any prior selection) and drag to
move; on empty desktop: deselect all.
.TP 8
.B B1-drag
on a selected icon: move the whole current selection in lock step.
.TP 8
.B B2
anywhere: post the context menu (see below).
.TP 8
.B B3-drag
on empty desktop: rubber-band selection; every icon whose
bounding box overlaps the dashed rectangle becomes selected on
release.
.TP 8
.B Double-B1
on an icon: activate it. If the icon's path is a RISC OS-style
.B !App
folder (a directory whose name begins with
.B !
containing a
.B !Boot
file), pinboard forks a private namespace, applies the app's
.B !Boot
script, sets
.B $approot
and
.B $wmicon
in the environment, and runs
.BI !Run .
Any other path is handed to
.IR plumber (1)
as a text message; the plumb rules decide which viewer or editor
opens it.
.PP
Drags initiated in
.IR wm-filer (1)
that miss every filer window are forwarded to
.B /chan/pinboard
as
.B pin
commands at the drop point.
.SH "CONTEXT MENU"
Posted by B2, the menu offers:
.TP
.B Remove
Delete every currently selected icon from the pinboard.
.TP
.B "Clear backdrop"
Drop the current backdrop image, exposing the canvas's solid bg.
.TP
.B Tidy
Re-flow every pin onto a row-major grid based on the current
screen rectangle.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /chan/pinboard
The text-protocol file2chan. Created at startup and removed when
.I pinboard
exits.
.TP
.B /lib/wmsetup
Standard wm initialisation script; should
.B wmrun
.B wm/pinboard
right after starting
.IR plumber (1).
.TP
.B /usr/inferno/!Pinboard/
The
.B !App
folder for the pinboard itself, providing
.BR !Boot ,
.BR !Run ,
and
.BR icons/!Pinboard.bit / .mask .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/pinboard.b
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR wm (1),
.IR wm-filer (1),
.IR toolbar (1),
.IR plumber (1),
.IR plumbmsg (2),
.IR tkclient (2),
.IR image (6).
.SH BUGS
The
.B backdrop
verb only accepts native Inferno
.B .bit
images. The
.IR wm (1)
in this tree exposes a
.B Sticky
client flag specifically so the pinboard can stay at the back of
the z-order; running
.I pinboard
under a stock wm will let pointer events keep raising it to the
front.
.PP
The
.B !App
icons shipped under
.B /usr/inferno/!*
often have rectangular
.B .mask
files that don't follow the icon's actual silhouette. When such an
icon is pinned, its internal background colour fills the bounding
box. Use the
.B maskify
helper in
.B /home/caerwyn/github/icons/
to regenerate per-icon masks by chroma-keying against the icon's
corner pixel.
